readme.http -- the HTTP server, and serving pages from a module
================================================================

The HTTP server is the httpd/main module: Mongoose, on a thread of its
own, with modules claiming the paths they serve.  They never see a socket.
The design is ircu2's (doc/readme.http in ircu2), adapted to Services.

    modules/httpd/http.h            what every other module sees
    modules/httpd/main/server.c     the server thread; the only file that
                                    knows Mongoose exists
    modules/httpd/main/routes.c     the routes, the requests waiting for
                                    an answer, their deadline
    modules/httpd/main/util.c       building answers, reading requests
    modules/httpd/main/main.c       the configuration, start and stop
    vendor/mongoose/                Mongoose 7.23, never edited

The settings (ListenTo, TLSCertificate, TLSKey, MaxConnections,
RequestTimeout) are in readme.modules; what the modules that come with
Services do is in readme.features, section 3-6.

1. Why a library
----------------

This replaced a hand-written parser that worked.  The argument is not that
it was broken; it is that HTTP is a protocol where being nearly right is a
security bug, and the list of ways to get framing wrong -- chunked
encoding, folded headers, pipelining, Content-Length against
Transfer-Encoding -- is a list somebody else has already walked, with
fuzzers, for twenty years.  TLS came with it.

The rule that follows: vendor/mongoose/ is upstream's amalgamation and is
never edited.  A local patch would give away the only thing it is there
for.  Updating is replacing the two files and running the tests.
Mongoose is GPL-2.0-only, so the httpd/main module is distributed under
GPL-2.0 rather than "2 or later"; see vendor/mongoose/README.

2. Claiming a path
------------------

A module that serves pages lists httpd/main in its ModuleInfo.requires
(readme.mod_api), so that it is loaded after it and httpd/main cannot be
unloaded from under it, and claims routes from its `init':

    http_add_route(module, "GET", "/nick/", handle_nick, NULL);

A path ending in `/' matches everything under it; anything else matches
exactly.  An exact match beats a prefix, and a longer prefix beats a
shorter one:

    /stats          matches only that
    /nick/          matches /nick/anything
    /               matches every path nothing else claimed

That is the whole routing language, on purpose.  A route for "/" is
therefore a catch-all, and httpd/top-page answers 404 itself for any path
other than "/".  The method is part of the route; a route for GET answers
HEAD too (the body is not sent).  Two claims on the same method and path
are refused.  A request nothing claimed is answered 404 by httpd/main.

A module gives its routes up from its `fini', with
http_del_routes(module); httpd/main also drops them when the module
is unloaded, and lets go of the requests that were waiting on them.  A
route may be claimed while nothing is listening, and http_available() says
whether anything ever will (a ListenTo is configured): a module that finds
nothing will reach its pages says so in the log and carries on.

3. Answering, now or later
--------------------------

A handler runs in the main thread:

    static int handle(http_req_t id, const struct HttpRequest *req,
                      struct HttpResponse *res, void *user)

It either fills in `res' and returns nonzero, or keeps `id', returns zero,
and answers later:

    struct HttpResponse *res = http_response(id);   /* NULL: gone */
    if (res) {
        http_response_set(res, 200, "text/html", NULL, 0);
        http_response_printf(res, "<p>%s</p>", html);
        http_respond(id, NULL);
    }

Answering later is what a handler does when it needs a record that may
not be in memory.  Anybody on the Internet can ask for any path, so a
page that read the database with the main thread waiting would let them
stop Services at will.  httpd/redirect is the example: it asks for the
record with store_prefetch() (through prefetch_nickinfo() and friends,
with its own THIS_MODULE as the owner, so that the call is dropped if it
is unloaded), and answers from the callback, when the record is in
memory.  The callback may run before prefetch returns, when the record
was in memory already.

Every request has a deadline, RequestTimeout (5 seconds by default).  One
that passes is answered "504 Gateway Timeout", and a later http_respond()
for it does nothing: a module that answers late has been slow, not
wrong.

What a handler can build an answer with:

    http_response_set()       status, type, body (replacing)
    http_response_printf()    append to the body (also _write, _vprintf)
    http_response_header()    one more header
    http_response_error()     an HTML error page
    http_response_redirect()  a 3xx with Location
    http_response_file()      a file, streamed by the server thread

and read the request with http_request_header() and http_request_var()
(a variable of the query string or of an urlencoded form, decoded).  The
body a handler writes may be up to 4 MiB; a bigger answer is a file.  A
response header holding a newline is dropped when it is sent: a newline
there is how one response becomes two.

4. Access control
-----------------

Before a request is routed, httpd/main emits its "httpd.auth" event
(HTTPD_EVENT_AUTH in http.h), which httpd/auth-ip and httpd/auth-password
attach to (event_attach(), readme.mod_api):

    int handler(const struct HttpRequest *req, struct HttpResponse *res)

returning HTTP_AUTH_UNDECIDED (let the next one decide), HTTP_AUTH_ALLOW
(let it through), or HTTP_AUTH_DENY after filling in `res' with the
refusal.  It runs for every request, whether anything claimed its path or
not, so that the answer to a stranger does not say what exists.

5. Which thread is which
------------------------

    the sockets, the parsing, TLS    the server thread
    the routes, the handlers         the main thread
    a request going up               worker_post(): the server thread
                                     posts it, and it is routed in the
                                     main thread on the next drain
    an answer coming down            an outbox under a mutex, and
                                     mg_wakeup() to wake the poll

Across that line travels a structure of bytes and a connection id -- an
integer Mongoose guarantees unique -- never a pointer into either side.
A connection that went away while its answer was being worked out is
simply not found.

ircu2 passes the answer itself through mg_wakeup().  On POSIX that is a
datagram sent without waiting and dropped, silently, when its socket
buffer is full -- and the answer and its memory with it.  Here mg_wakeup()
is only a doorbell, and the outbox is emptied after every poll, so a lost
wake-up costs at most one poll interval (200 ms).

Nothing on the server thread touches Services' state: server.c does not
include services.h (see include/worker.h), and Mongoose's own logging is
compiled out; what it has to say about a failed listen comes back as a
task the main thread logs.

6. What it will not read
------------------------

Mongoose does the framing.  What it deliberately does not do is decide
what a path means, and that decision is server.c's:

    ".." in a path        400.  Nothing in httpd/main opens a file, but
                          a path is handed to modules and one of them
                          may, so the check belongs where the path is
                          first believed.
    "%2F", "%5C"          400.  Routing happens on the decoded path, so an
                          encoded separator would turn what the client
                          wrote as data into a separator and reach a
                          prefix route it is not under.
    "%00"                 400.  It shortens the path for whoever reads it
                          with str*() next.
    a control byte        400.
    a body over 64 KiB    413, at the headers when the request says how
                          long it is, before any of the body is read; a
                          chunked body is refused as soon as more than
                          that has arrived.  The connection is closed.

The path a handler is given is decoded; the query string is not, because
"+" means a space there and nowhere else.  http_request_var() decodes a
variable when it is asked for.

7. TLS
------

A ListenTo followed by ", tls" is an HTTPS port, with the PEM files of
TLSCertificate and TLSKey (both or neither).  Which TLS depends on the
build (SERVICES_HTTP_TLS, BUILDING.md):

    openssl     Mongoose uses OpenSSL: any certificate OpenSSL takes.
                The default ("auto") when OpenSSL is installed.
    builtin     Mongoose's own: self-contained, TLS 1.3 only and ECDSA
                certificates only.  An RSA certificate is refused at the
                handshake, which from the client looks like the
                connection being dropped.

    openssl req -x509 -newkey ec -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:prime256v1 \
      -keyout http-key.pem -out http-cert.pem -days 365 -nodes \
      -subj "/CN=services.example.net"

That asymmetry is worth knowing before it is diagnosed at three in the
morning: the same PEM files that work on an OpenSSL build fail on a
builtin one.

